.TH NIXALARM 1 "2026-11-20" "nixalarm 0.2.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nixalarm \- graphical Linux alarm clock with configurable audio sources
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nixalarm
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ HH:MM ]
.br
.B nixalarm
.B --test-source
.I NAME
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B nixalarm
.B --list-sources
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B nixalarm
.RI [ --config " PATH" ]
.B --configure-source
.I NAME
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nixalarm
displays the current local time in a configurable clock window and rings at
the next occurrence of the requested local time.
.PP
The alarm plays a generated tone, local audio files, internet streams, MIDI
files rendered through FluidSynth, or RTL-SDR radio (narrowband FM, broadcast
FM, AM) received through
.BR rtl_fm (1).
Local files and network streams are decoded through FFmpeg.
.PP
Configuration comes from
.BR nixalarm (5).
Command line options override the matching values for the current run.
Task-oriented guides ship in the repository under
.I docs/users/alarms.md
and
.IR docs/users/sources.md ;
they are not installed.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Configuration
.TP
.BI --config " PATH"
Read configuration from
.I PATH
instead of the default location.
A missing file is normal; see
.BR nixalarm (5)
for the default paths and the seeded defaults.
.TP
.BI --source " NAME"
Use the configured or built-in source named
.I NAME
for this run, overriding
.B alarm_source
from the configuration file.
An unknown name fails with exit status 2.
.TP
.B --fullscreen
Start in fullscreen desktop mode.
.TP
.B --windowed
Start windowed, overriding
.B fullscreen = true
from the configuration file.
.SS Standalone actions
.TP
.B --list-sources
Print the names and types of the built-in and configured sources, then exit.
It accepts
.B --config
to read a different file.
It arms no alarm, plays nothing, and checks no SDR gain.
.TP
.BI --test-source " NAME"
Play source
.I NAME
immediately instead of scheduling an alarm.
A command line time and the configured alarms are ignored.
Gain validation and fallback behave as for a scheduled alarm.
Playback ends when the alarm is snoozed or dismissed, or when
.B nixalarm
quits.
.TP
.BI --configure-source " NAME"
Run the read-only gain setup wizard for an existing SDR source or a built-in
preset.
Only
.B --config
may accompany this action, in any order; any other option or alarm argument is
rejected with exit status 2.
Standard input and standard error must be interactive terminals, while standard
output may be redirected.
Prompts and instructions go to standard error; only the suggested TOML goes to
standard output, and only after confirmation.
The wizard creates no files, edits no files, initializes no audio, accesses no
hardware, arms no alarm, and selects no alarm source.
For the wizard, the config path must name a readable regular file or a file
that does not yet exist.
.SS Informational
.TP
.B --help
Print command usage, then exit.
.TP
.B --version
Print the version, then exit.
.SH TIME FORMAT
Times without
.B AM
or
.B PM
use 24-hour local time with hours 0 through 23.
Times with
.B AM
or
.B PM
in any letter case, with or without periods, use 12-hour local time with hours
1 through 12.
Minutes are 00 through 59.
If the requested time has already passed today,
.B nixalarm
schedules the alarm for tomorrow.
Each alarm fires once and does not recur.
.B nixalarm
must still be running when the time arrives; it does not wake a suspended
machine.
.PP
Examples:
.EX
nixalarm 19:30
nixalarm "7:30 PM"
.EE
.PP
If no time is given,
.B nixalarm
uses the
.B alarms
list from
.BR nixalarm (5),
for example for desktop menu launches.
Set
.B alarms = []
for clock-only launches with no alarm.
An invalid entry in the configured list fails with exit status 2.
.PP
The
.B use_24_hour
key in
.BR nixalarm (5)
changes only the clock display.
It does not change how alarm times are parsed.
.SH CONTROLS
.TP
.B Space
While ringing, tap to snooze for the configured snooze period.
In source test mode, tapping ends playback.
.TP
.B Space hold
While ringing, hold continuously for the configured stop duration to dismiss
the alarm and end playback.
.TP
.B F11
Toggle fullscreen.
.TP
.B f
Toggle fullscreen.
.TP
.B q
Quit.
.TP
.B Escape
Quit.
.SH BUILT-IN SOURCES
.TP
.B generated
Procedural PCM alarm tone.
This is the default source.
It needs no files, network, or SDR hardware, and it never falls back to
another source.
.TP
.B weatherband_162_400
.TQ
.B weatherband_162_425
.TQ
.B weatherband_162_450
.TQ
.B weatherband_162_475
.TQ
.B weatherband_162_500
.TQ
.B weatherband_162_525
.TQ
.B weatherband_162_550
RTL-SDR presets, one per weather-band channel from 162.400 to 162.550 MHz in
25 kHz steps.
Coverage is location-dependent, so the presets name channels, not
transmitters; each names the channel that covers the receiver location.
For reception without SDR hardware, add an internet mirror as a
.B [sources.NAME]
section of type
.B internet
in
.BR nixalarm (5).
.PP
Each SDR preset needs an explicit manual
.B gain
in the configuration before an alarm is armed or a source test starts.
Missing gain and the legacy
.B gain = "auto"
setting are rejected with exit status 2.
The
.B --configure-source
wizard prints instructions for adding or replacing the setting.
Normal launches never prompt.
Clock-only runs and
.B --list-sources
never check gain.
See
.BR nixalarm (5)
for source definitions and gain validation.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Ring with the default generated alarm at 07:30:
.EX
$ nixalarm 07:30
.EE
.TP
Ring at 7:30 in the evening:
.EX
$ nixalarm "7:30 PM"
.EE
.TP
Start fullscreen with the configured default source:
.EX
$ nixalarm --fullscreen 07:30
.EE
.TP
Test the generated alarm source immediately:
.EX
$ nixalarm --test-source generated
.EE
.TP
Generate setup instructions for an RTL-SDR weather-band channel:
.EX
$ nixalarm --configure-source weatherband_162_400
.EE
.TP
Use the channel after applying the printed configuration:
.EX
$ nixalarm --source weatherband_162_400 07:30
.EE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful completion, including listing sources, printing help or version,
and generating wizard instructions.
.TP
.B 1
Runtime or input/output failure, or wizard cancellation, end of input, or
output error.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid invocation, unknown source, or invalid or missing SDR gain.
The wizard also returns this status when standard input or standard error has
no interactive terminal, when the source is unknown or is not an SDR source,
or when the source name uses characters outside the wizard's supported set
(ASCII letters, digits, '_' and '-').
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base directory of the default configuration file.
When unset or empty, the
.B HOME
fallback below is used.
.TP
.B HOME
Base directory of the fallback configuration path
.I $HOME/.config/nixalarm/config.toml
when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset or empty.
.TP
.B NIXALARM_ASSET_DIR
Optional directory of staged face artwork, checked before the installed and
development locations.
The nixie face treats it as the tube-art layout directory, while the dial
faces treat it as the surface-map directory.
When unset or empty, only the standard locations are searched.
Missing nixie artwork leaves a vignette-only frame; missing dial maps fall
back to procedural surfaces.
.TP
.B PATH
Used to locate the
.B rtl_fm
program for SDR sources.
A graphical launch may not inherit a shell search path.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/nixalarm/config.toml
Primary configuration file.
.TP
.I $HOME/.config/nixalarm/config.toml
Fallback configuration file when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset or empty.
.PP
See
.BR nixalarm (5)
for the file format, the seeded defaults, and the remaining lookup rules.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR nixalarm (5),
.BR rtl_fm (1),
.BR ffmpeg (1)
